From 193faaa4759c1ac8f2bcdc1f9e799f477d2acd00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 02:54:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 55/79] input: say what sizes the repeat cushion

A quarter of a second sitting next to a repeat invites two questions - why
not ask the demuxer, and is it enough - and the answers are measurable, so
write them down beside it rather than leaving the figure to be argued over
again.

Stalling the process at the instant the repeat is issued shows the tail to
be a budget for the stall: forty milliseconds of it carried an eighty
millisecond stall and dropped audio at a hundred, a quarter of a second
carried three hundred and dropped at three hundred and fifty, half a second
carried five hundred. The demuxer restart the cushion is nominally for is
the small term, so a demuxer hint would parameterise the wrong thing, and
measuring the last pass would take the size from the passes that came back
promptly rather than from the one that did not.

The other half is what the figure costs and what caps it. It costs the
position report, which runs ahead of the sound by whatever is left unplayed,
and it is capped by the read-ahead, so on a caching smaller than the lead
the repeat already fires at the end of the file and takes the tail as it
finds it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/input/input.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/input/input.c b/src/input/input.c
index d7e3fc619c..0af3384639 100644
--- a/src/input/input.c
+++ b/src/input/input.c
@@ -710,7 +710,16 @@ static int MainLoopTryRepeat( input_thread_t *p_input )
 }
 
 /* How much of the tail the output may still be holding when the next pass is
- * asked for. */
+ * asked for.
+ *
+ * It is a budget for the stall rather than an estimate of the restart: the
+ * restart is the small term - ogg is reading again in about a millisecond - so
+ * there is nothing a demuxer could advertise that would size this, and taking
+ * it from what the last pass cost would size it from the passes that did not
+ * need it. The read-ahead bounds it from above, so a lead beyond the caching
+ * just takes whatever the tail turns out to be at the end of the file. What it
+ * costs is the position report, which runs ahead of the sound by whatever is
+ * left unplayed. */
 #define INPUT_REPEAT_LEAD (CLOCK_FREQ / 4)
 
 /**
